@@@ hint
For cell `(i, j)` (0-based) in a grid of size `m = 2n - 1`, how far is it from the nearest edge? The four distances are `i`, `j`, `m-1-i` and `m-1-j`.
@@@ hint
The ring index is `d = min(i, j, m-1-i, m-1-j)` (0 for the outer ring), so the value is `n - d`. Print it with `printf("%*d", w, n - d)`.
@@@ solution_code
#include <stdio.h>

static int min2(int a, int b) { return a < b ? a : b; }

int main(void)
{
    int n;
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n <= 0) return 0;

    int m = 2 * n - 1;              /* grid size */
    int w = 0;
    for (int v = n; v > 0; v /= 10)
        w++;                        /* width of the largest value */

    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
            /* distance to the nearest border = ring index (0 = outer) */
            int d = min2(min2(i, j), min2(m - 1 - i, m - 1 - j));
            if (j > 0) putchar(' ');
            printf("%*d", w, n - d);
        }
        putchar('\n');
    }
    return 0;
}
@@@ explanation_md
**Approach.** Every ring is the set of cells at the same distance from the border. For cell `(i, j)` in an `m x m` grid (`m = 2n - 1`), the distance to the nearest edge is `d = min(i, j, m-1-i, m-1-j)`. The outer ring has `d = 0` and the center has `d = n - 1`. The printed value is `n - d`, so the grid needs no storage and each cell costs O(1).

```
n = 3, m = 5, cell (1, 3):
i = 1, j = 3, m-1-i = 3, m-1-j = 1  ->  d = 1  ->  value 2
```

**Complexity.** Time O(n^2) (O(1) per cell for `(2n-1)^2` cells). Space O(1).

**Common mistakes**
- Filling a 2D array ring by ring. It works, but costs O(n^2) memory, which is not acceptable on a microcontroller with a few KB of RAM, and needs careful loop bounds.
- Using `max` instead of `min`, or forgetting one of the four distances. The grid is then only correct in one quadrant.
- Using `m - i` instead of `m - 1 - i` (off by one). The rings come out asymmetric.
- Ignoring the width: for `n >= 10` the columns misalign unless every value uses `%*d` with `w = 2`.

**Interview follow-ups**
- Generalize to an `R x C` rectangle. What is the value in the center when `min(R, C)` is even?
- Print the pattern for `n = 10^9`, but only rows `r1..r2` and columns `c1..c2`. Why is the closed form now mandatory?
- Print the **distance from the center** instead (`max(|i - c|, |j - c|) + 1`, the Chebyshev distance). Why is it the same picture?
